# Ward — environment contract (single file, repo root).
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill EVERY variable. `api/src/config.ts` validates the whole
# set with zod at import time and the process exits non-zero, naming the offending
# variable, if anything is missing or malformed. There are no silent fallbacks:
# atrium's D29 exists because an app in this estate booted with an empty library
# and never told anyone, and Ward is the service six apps authenticate against.
#
# Every variable below names its READER — the module that actually consumes it.
# A settable variable that changes nothing costs the next person an afternoon,
# so if a name here stops having a reader, delete the name.
#
# In production these come from pm2's environment rather than this file; a
# missing `.env` on the server is normal. The schema check is the real gate.


# --- Listener --------------------------------------------------------------
# Reader: api/src/index.ts (binds the Fastify listener)

# Loopback port Caddy reverse-proxies `/ward-api/*` to. 8787, 8788, 8790, 8794
# and 8080 already belong to other projects on the shared VPS; the deploy
# (brief 11) fixes the real one.
PORT=8791

# Optional — the one variable with a default, and it defaults to loopback.
#
# Atrium binds 0.0.0.0, so its "Caddy is the only way in" claim rests entirely
# on the VPS firewall: one bad `ufw` rule and the identity service for six apps
# answers the internet directly, past every header and rate limit Caddy applies.
# Ward binds 127.0.0.1 instead, so the kernel refuses off-host connections
# whether or not the firewall is right, and the two controls have to fail
# together. Set 0.0.0.0 only in a container, and only on purpose.
# HOST=127.0.0.1


# --- Database --------------------------------------------------------------
# Reader: api/src/db/connection.ts (brief 01) — the better-sqlite3 file

# Relative values resolve against the REPO ROOT, not the working directory, so
# pm2 and a hand-run script cannot end up on two different database files.
# Holds accounts, credentials, sessions and grants — back it up; it is the only
# copy of who anybody is.
WARD_DB_PATH=api/data/ward.db


# --- Superuser (break-glass) -----------------------------------------------
# Reader: the console session code (brief 06)
#
# This is NOT the admin account you use day to day. It is the break-glass
# credential from wiki/decisions-admin.md: it has no row in any table, no
# subject and no grants, so it reaches Ward's admin console and nothing else —
# not atrium, not newspapper, not prm. Daily administration belongs to the
# OWNER ACCOUNT, an ordinary account with explicit grants, created through the
# console at cutover. Conflating the two is the specific mistake that decisions
# page exists to prevent.
#
# Read this as what it is: a live credential sitting in plaintext on disk,
# permanently. That was accepted deliberately — it is the only thing that still
# works when the database is empty, corrupted, or has had its last admin
# removed, which is exactly what a recovery mechanism has to survive. Give it a
# long random password, do not reuse it anywhere, and expect to use it roughly
# never. Rotating it is an edit here plus a restart, not a UI action; that
# friction is correct for a credential of this kind.
WARD_ADMIN_USERNAME=change-me
WARD_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-to-something-long-and-random


# --- Token signing ---------------------------------------------------------
# Reader: api/src/tokens/ (brief 02)

# The Ed25519 private key Ward alone holds — it signs every access token in the
# estate; the apps get only the public half via /.well-known/jwks.json.
#
# Relative values resolve against the repo root. The key file itself is NOT in
# the repo and NOT in this file: on the server it is created once, backed up,
# and never regenerated by a deploy, because a new key signs the whole estate
# out at once and reads as an outage. Keep it mode 0600.
WARD_SIGNING_KEY_PATH=api/data/signing-key.pem


# --- Public origin ---------------------------------------------------------
# Readers: token minting (brief 02) as the `iss` claim, and the UI for redirects

# The estate's single public origin, in the browser's sense: scheme + host +
# optional port, and NOTHING else. Not the API base URL — `https://gandolh.ro`,
# never `https://gandolh.ro/ward-api`. A value carrying a path is rejected
# rather than trimmed, because getting this wrong produces tokens whose `iss`
# looks plausible and cookies scoped to the wrong place.
#
# `iss` is compared as an exact string by six independent verifiers, so a
# trailing slash here would reject every token in the estate. Trailing slashes
# are stripped for you; anything else about the value has to be right.
#
# In development this is your local origin, e.g. http://localhost:5173.
WARD_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://gandolh.ro

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mail — verification for public registration (brief 07)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by `api/src/mail/` via `MAIL` in api/src/config.ts.
#
# The mode is explicit and required. It is deliberately NOT inferred from
# NODE_ENV: an estate whose mail silently stopped leaving the building because
# a variable was unset somewhere is a registration flow that looks like it
# works and delivers nothing. Ward would rather refuse to boot and name the
# variable than fail at the moment somebody is waiting for a link.
#
#   file  — write messages to WARD_MAIL_FILE_DIR instead of sending them.
#           Needs no SMTP account, which is what makes the whole flow
#           exercisable locally. The directory is gitignored, because a
#           verification mail holds a single-use token.
#   smtp  — actually send, via the four WARD_SMTP_* settings below.
WARD_MAIL_TRANSPORT=file

# Required when WARD_MAIL_TRANSPORT=file. Relative to the repo root.
WARD_MAIL_FILE_DIR=api/mail-outbox

# Required when WARD_MAIL_TRANSPORT=smtp. Unset in file mode.
#WARD_SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.net
#WARD_SMTP_PORT=587
#WARD_SMTP_USER=ward@gandolh.ro
#WARD_SMTP_PASSWORD=change-me

# The From: address. Required in BOTH modes — a message written to disk has to
# look like the one that would have been sent, or `file` stops being a
# rehearsal of the real thing.
WARD_MAIL_FROM=ward@gandolh.ro
